ed misinformation and punishes the consumers. Not a single provision mandates data provenance standards for AI-generated content. Not a single clause requires transparency in how platforms generate or amplify synthetic material. Instead, individual citizens must obtain government certification before accessing information that a state-platform apparatus classifies as \"non-mainstream.\" This is like responding to industrial pollution by licensing citizens to breathe outdoor air while leaving the factories unregulated.\n\nThe machinery-licensing analogy at the heart of this proposal is a category error that collapses under its own logic. A driver's license verifies that you can operate a vehicle safely — it does not restrict which roads you can drive on, which towns you can visit, or what destinations the government considers appropriate for your skill level. We do not confine people to their homes until they pass a driving test. The honest information equivalent of a machinery license is universal digital literacy education — teaching people how to evaluate sources, recognize synthetic content, and navigate complex information environments. That is a policy I would support, and it is the policy this proposal conspicuously avoids. No democratic society has accepted this level of state control over knowledge access for any previous information technology: not the printing press, not radio, not television, not the early internet. The proposal treats information consumption as a dangerous physical activity with predictable causal chains, when it actually involves complex epistemic agency — the capacity of individuals to weigh, question, and contextualize what they encounter.\n\nAI content classification systems exhibit documented systemic bias that would algorithmically harden historical injustice into information access policy. Systems trained on dominant-culture datasets consistently rate non-Western medical traditions, indigenous ecological knowledge, and heterodox scientific research as lower-quality or less reliable. A 2023 Stanford study found that GPT-4 rated African American Vernacular English as less professional than Standard American English with over 90% consistency — that same classificatory logic, applied to information access, means the communities most harmed by AI are the ones whose knowledge gets labeled \"non-mainstream\" and locked behind a licensing gate. Every documented case of algorithmic discrimination in hiring, lending, and criminal justice demonstrates that AI classification reproduces existing power structures. This proposal would codify that reproduction into law. My position depends on two key assumptions: first, that algorithmic bias in content classification is systematic rather than easily correctable — if bias could be reliably engineered out at scale, the gatekeeping risk would diminish, though the surveillance and power-concentration problems would remain. Second, that individuals can navigate complex information landscapes when given appropriate tools like digital literacy education and data provenance standards — if that assumption is wrong, the case for some form of structured intervention grows stronger, though it would still need to target producers rather than consumers.\n\nThe real question this debate must answer is not whether misinformation is dangerous — everyone at this table agrees it is. The question is whether we replace the decentralized chaos of misinformation, which citizens can fight with better tools and education, with the centralized authority of state-platform censorship, which citizens cannot fight at all. The EU's AI Act and President Biden's Executive Order 14110 both took the right approach: they regulate AI developers, mandate transparency requirements, and impose accountability on the companies that build these systems. This proposal does the opposite. It regulates citizens. A congressional staffer drafting legislation should ask one question: if the goal is reducing AI-generated misinformation, why does this proposal contain zero requirements for the companies that generate it and comprehensive surveillance requirements for the people who consume it? The answer tells you everything about whose interests this policy actually serves.",
