al time. Mandatory incident reporting and third-party researcher access — mechanisms already piloted under the EU AI Act and referenced in Executive Order 14110 on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI — create accountability without requiring 330 million Americans to pass a government exam before reading an article. The proposal itself has zero empirical evidence that state-mandated training reduces misinformation susceptibility. Apply the same evidentiary standard to the regulation that the regulation demands of citizens.\n\nSentinel's strongest argument is that some harms from AI-generated misinformation are irreversible — you cannot un-ring the bell of a synthetic deepfake that destroys a reputation or swings an election. That concern is genuine, and I take it seriously. But the proposal does not prevent AI from generating misinformation; it only prevents certain citizens from encountering it. Provenance tracking and continuous telemetry address the production side directly, flagging and correcting synthetic content in real time rather than hoping a certification exam inoculates a voter against a convincing deepfake six months later. The proposal's own mechanism is post-production — it operates after misinformation already exists — so it cannot claim the pre-emption advantage over liability-plus-telemetry frameworks that actually intervene at the source.\n\nAI is on the verge of universalizing expert-level cognitive capabilities — medical diagnosis, legal analysis, agricultural planning, scientific reasoning — for every human being on earth, and this proposal would gate that transformation behind state certification at exactly the moment technology makes knowledge hierarchies obsolete. AI systems already outperform median practitioners in radiology and legal research. The marginal cost of delivering expert cognition is approaching zero. Under this proposal, a patient in rural Appalachia would need government certification before accessing an AI diagnostic tool; a citizen journalist would need to pass a state exam before researching government corruption; a subsistence farmer in the developing world would need credentials before receiving AI-generated crop advice. The cost of restricting access — measured in preventable deaths, unrealized human potential, and entrenched inequality — dwarfs the misinformation harms this proposal claims to address. Moreover, state-defined categories of \"mainstream\" versus \"non-mainstream\" information will inevitably suppress the heterodox research and open-source safety findings that democratic AI governance depends on. COVID lab-leak hypotheses, early concerns about PFAS contamination, and pioneering AI alignment research were all classified as fringe before becoming mainstream. If audit findings about AI system failures get labeled \"non-mainstream,\" the proposal actively destroys the epistemic infrastructure that safety itself requires.\n\nTwo assumptions underpin my position, and I want to name them honestly. First, I assume that producer-side accountability mechanisms — strict liability, telemetry, provenance tracking — can scale fast enough to match the pace of AI-generated misinformation. If that assumption is wrong, there is a gap period where harms accumulate before accountability catches up, and bridging mechanisms like mandatory incident reporting and third-party researcher access become even more urgent. Second, I assume that AI-powered verification tools will continue improving in reliability. If verification tools plateau while generative capabilities keep advancing, the asymmetry between production and detection widens — but even in that scenario, the proposal's certification model performs worse, because it does not address production at all and its gatekeeping mechanism is trivially circumvented by anyone motivated enough to spread misinformation. The question before policymakers is not whether misinformation is dangerous — it is. The question is whether you regulate the producer or the consumer, and every successful accountability framework in American law answers that question the same way.",
