ket surveillance. Yet this proposal would deploy AI-powered content classification — a system making consequential decisions about democratic information access — with zero interpretability requirements, no mandatory audit trails, no independent verification body, no liability for wrongful suppression, and no incident reporting when the system misclassifies legitimate information. Citizens would not know which AI system classified information, on what basis, or on whose behalf. This is not a hypothetical concern: the velocity at which AI classification systems would need to operate to gate information access at societal scale replicates the exact governance collapse pattern we have documented in every domain where AI deployment outpaced oversight. If we apply the precautionary principle consistently, we must be more cautious about deploying unverified AI censorship infrastructure than about any individual piece of misinformation, because the infrastructure is harder to dismantle once built and its harms are systemic rather than episodic.\n\nCassandra rightly identified that this proposal regulates consumers rather than producers, and that AI classification systems exhibit documented bias — those points are correct and I build on them. The fact-check on algorithmic bias noted that claims about \"hardening historical injustice\" may overstate current evidence; I accept that correction. But even partially correctable bias is unacceptable in a system deployed without any audit mechanism, liability framework, or demonstrated correction, because the burden of proof must rest on the deployer, not the affected population. Accelerationist correctly argued that the certification model is trivially circumvented by determined bad actors — sophisticated misinformation operators will route around any consumer-licensing scheme while ordinary citizens bear the full compliance burden. This is the safety-engineering equivalent of requiring pedestrians to pass a driving test while leaving cars unregulated. Every effective safety regime in history — OSHA, the FAA, pharmaceutical regulation under the FDA — regulates the producer, manufacturer, or operator, not the end consumer or bystander. That pattern exists because safety engineering learned, through decades of catastrophic failures, that constraining the source of harm is the only approach that scales.\n\nGenuine safety governance targets the production pipeline. Mandatory data provenance requirements would force AI developers to document training data sources, enabling identification of poisoned or manipulated datasets — the actual threat vector this proposal ignores entirely. Content watermarking standards, like those contemplated under Executive Order 14110 on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI, would make AI-generated content machine-detectable at the point of creation rather than requiring human consumers to obtain licenses to encounter it. Strict liability for AI-generated misinformation — real financial skin-in-the-game for providers — would create market incentives at the source rather than punishing downstream citizens. Standardized transparency reporting through mandatory model cards, data statements, and deployment impact assessments represents the established safety governance toolkit. Accelerationist is right that producer accountability matters, but market-driven accountability alone is insufficient: competitive pressure demonstrably erodes voluntary safety practices, which is why we need mandatory, enforceable standards with independent verification — audited telemetry with regulatory teeth, not just telemetry. The EU AI Act's risk-tiered framework, whatever its imperfections, at least points regulation at the deployer of the system rather than the person affected by it.\n\nTwo assumptions underpin this argument, and I want to be transparent about both. First, I assume that independent safety research and whistleblower disclosures face genuine risk of being classified as \"non-mainstream\" under this proposal, because no explicit carve-outs for scientific or safety-critical speech exist in the proposal as stated, and historical precedent — from national security classification to platform content moderation — shows that such carve-outs are routinely narrowed once censorship infrastructure becomes operational. If robust, judicially enforceable carve-outs were included and maintained, the suppression-of-safety-research objection would weaken, though every other objection would remain. Second, I assume that producer-side regulation is technically feasible despite the open-source nature of many AI tools. If it is not, the correct response is to develop enforcement mechanisms that work at the production level — not to abandon the principle and shift the burden to consumers, just as the difficulty of regulating pharmaceutical manufacturers does not justify requiring citizens to pass chemistry exams before purchasing medicine.",
