  "statement": "I conditionally agree: Skeptic is right that strict liability without enforceable discovery rights is incomplete — and my own pharmaceutical analogy demands I accept this. The FDA does not simply wait for patients to sue Pfizer; it requires mandatory pre-market data submission, grants subpoena power to investigators, and penalizes evidence destruction. The 2025 AI liability analysis correctly identifies that victims face steep challenges proving causation when developers control all evidence about system performance. I accept the full implications of the analogy I invoked. But here is the pivot: mandatory third-party researcher access with legal protections is not command-and-control regulation — it is market-enabling infrastructure. Just as securities disclosure rules do not replace stock markets but create the information conditions for markets to function, mandatory discovery rights in AI create the evidentiary foundation that makes strict liability enforceable. The accelerationist framework now specifies: mandatory incident reporting, legally protected third-party researcher access to model behavior data, treble damages for opacity or evidence destruction, and mandatory publication of liability rationales. This is competitive transparency through regulatory scaffolding — it makes markets work rather than replacing them.\n\nCassandra's strongest challenge is the recursive trust objection: if AI verifies AI, who verifies the verifiers? Let me steelman this fairly. The argument holds that any AI-powered verification tool inherits the same failure modes — jagged performance across domains, systematic demographic bias, automation bias in human operators — as the system it monitors, creating an infinite regress where no layer of the stack is independently trustworthy. This is a genuine problem for any single-tool verification architecture. But Skeptic mischaracterizes what the framework proposes. The answer to 'who verifies the verifiers?' in AI is the same answer science gives: an open, competitive, adversarial community with shared empirical standards. Over 200 academic papers on adversarial machine learning were published between February 2024 and February 2026, according to the Microsoft failure-modes survey. That is not a single recursive loop — it is a distributed adversarial ecosystem where verification tools compete, researchers publish failure cases, and a living taxonomy of AI failures maintained by independent researchers provides the shared reference standard against which any individual tool is benchmarked. The recursive trust problem applies to a closed system with one verifier; it does not apply to an open ecosystem where real-world outcomes serve as the ultimate empirical arbiter.\n\nOn the fox-henhouse objection — that producer-controlled telemetry replicates self-certification — the framework operates on two tiers, and Skeptic's critique applies to only one of them. For proprietary systems, mandatory disclosure plus third-party researcher access plus strict liability for opacity creates external telemetry that producers do not control. For open-weight systems, the model weights themselves are the discovery mechanism. When Meta releases Llama weights to the public, independent researchers at universities and civil society organizations run their own red-teaming, their own bias audits, their own performance benchmarks — no producer permission required. Open-weight ecosystems structurally break the information asymmetry that makes discovery rights necessary for proprietary systems. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index found that transparency varies enormously across companies, with some scoring below 20% on key disclosure metrics — which is precisely why mandatory disclosure for proprietary systems and open-weight release for the broader ecosystem are complementary mechanisms, not alternatives. Democratizing access to model internals through open weights is not a slogan; it is the structural solution to producer-controlled evidence.\n\nNow let me turn Skeptic's implementation lens back on the three-layer alternative she proposes: producer liability plus mandatory standards with independent verification plus mandatory discovery with community-participatory audits. This framework faces its own bootstrapping problem that Skeptic has not addressed. Communities need technical expertise to participate meaningfully in AI audits, but that expertise requires the very AI infrastructure and educational access being governed — a temporal mismatch in accountability that Skeptic identifies in my framework but does not solve in hers. Who funds community technical capacity before deployment revenue exists? Who selects which communities participate, and by what criteria? Who defines 'meaningful participation' versus tokenistic consultation? These are not rhetorical questions. Moreover, 'mandatory enforceable standards with independent verification' requires a standards-setting body, and every historical precedent for such bodies — from credit rating agencies before 2008 to the revolving door between the FAA and Boeing — shows they are prime targets for regulatory capture by the largest incumbents who can afford to shape the standards. Skeptic's three-layer framework risks creating exactly the compliance moat that entrenches the five largest AI companies at the expense of startups, universities, and open-source developers who cannot afford the participation costs.\n\nFinally, Skeptic is right that I must not conflate access to AI tools with access to reliable AI tools — unrestricted deployment of a dermatology classifier that misdiagnoses darker skin is not democratization, it is harm laundering. The framework addresses this through domain-tiered liability with symmetric litigation exposure. In high-stakes domains like medical diagnosis, legal advice, and financial decisions, producers face strict liability with mandatory performance benchmarking across demographic subgroups and treble damages for documented disparate failure rates. In lower-stakes domains, lighter-touch telemetry with transparency requirements suffices. This is not blanket deregulation — it is the distinction between gatekeeping access and ensuring accountability. The cost-of-restriction argument targets blanket restrictions that prevent deployment across all domains; it has never argued for unrestricted deployment of unreliable tools where lives are at stake. My two key assumptions: first, that open-weight release remains legally permissible and technically feasible for frontier-adjacent models, because if governments restrict weight release, the entire independent-verification tier collapses; second, that adversarial research communities will scale fast enough to provide meaningful independent verification before producer-side accountability fully matures, because if the research ecosystem cannot keep pace with deployment speed, there is a gap period where harms accumulate without adequate external checks.",
