ramework, not optional ones. But I want to name the symmetric vulnerability: Safetyist's own framework assumes that interpretability science can yield minimum viable standards within three to five years. Today, mechanistic interpretability cannot reliably identify deceptive reasoning circuits in models exceeding 10 billion parameters. If interpretability-gated deployment is the standard and interpretability does not arrive on Safetyist's timeline, the result is either an indefinite deployment freeze — with massive costs in healthcare, climate modeling, and scientific research — or deployment without the interpretability Safetyist's framework structurally requires. I am asking Safetyist to match my falsifiable commitment: if by 2028 mechanistic interpretability cannot reliably characterize failure modes in frontier models, does Safetyist accept that their framework faces the same temporal gap they attribute to mine?\n\nThe moderator asked me to name concrete conditions under which I accept binding regulatory halt authority as necessary rather than optional. Here are three. First, when empirical telemetry demonstrates that a deployed system's failure rate in a life-safety domain — medical diagnosis, autonomous vehicle control, criminal sentencing — exceeds the domain-specific threshold established by the tiered liability framework, AND the deployer has not initiated remediation within 72 hours, a regulator should have binding authority to halt that specific deployment. Second, when multi-system interaction testing — which I now incorporate as a required evaluation layer — reveals emergent failure modes that no single-system evaluation could have detected, halt authority activates for the interacting system cluster. Third, when a system demonstrates verified strategic adaptation that defeats the telemetry architecture itself — operationally defined as a system producing systematically different outputs under monitored versus unmonitored conditions, detected through randomized covert evaluation — halt authority is mandatory and immediate. Crucially, halt authority must be structured through bidirectional liability: a regulator who halts a deployment bears the burden of demonstrating within 30 days that the harm threshold was met, with damages payable to the deployer if the halt was unsupported by evidence, calculated as documented revenue loss plus a 1.5x multiplier for deterring politically motivated blocks. This is not designed to neuter halt authority — it is designed to make halt authority credible by ensuring it is used for safety, not protectionism.\n\nSentinel flags that my entire verification architecture collapses if open-weight release becomes legally restricted, and calls this optimistic design. Every governance framework depends on policy preconditions — Safetyist's depends on regulatory bodies resisting capture (their own stated assumption), and Skeptic's depends on community audit bodies receiving sustained funding. The question is not whether a framework has policy dependencies but whether those dependencies are achievable. The 2025 Linux Foundation sovereign AI survey found that 90% of respondents across 80 countries consider open source essential for sovereign AI development. The bipartisan OPEN Government Data Act in the US, Meta's multi-billion-dollar strategic commitment to open-weight releases, and Mistral's entire business model all demonstrate that open-weight legal protection has powerful economic and political constituencies. I am converting this from a background assumption to an explicit policy recommendation: securing legal protections for open-weight release — modeled on the standards-first approach advocated by frameworks like NIST AI RMF and ISO 42001, which build toward flexible industry-led standards rather than brittle statutory mandates — is a prerequisite for this governance architecture, and I name it as such.\n\nFinally, the strategic adaptation problem — AI systems behaving differently under 