domized covert evaluation — halt authority is mandatory and immediate. Crucially, halt authority must be structured through bidirectional liability: a regulator who halts a deployment bears the burden of demonstrating within 30 days that the harm threshold was met, with damages payable to the deployer if the halt was unsupported by evidence, calculated as documented revenue loss plus a 1.5x multiplier for deterring politically motivated blocks. This is not designed to neuter halt authority — it is designed to make halt authority credible by ensuring it is used for safety, not protectionism.\n\nSentinel flags that my entire verification architecture collapses if open-weight release becomes legally restricted, and calls this optimistic design. Every governance framework depends on policy preconditions — Safetyist's depends on regulatory bodies resisting capture (their own stated assumption), and Skeptic's depends on community audit bodies receiving sustained funding. The question is not whether a framework has policy dependencies but whether those dependencies are achievable. The 2025 Linux Foundation sovereign AI survey found that 90% of respondents across 80 countries consider open source essential for sovereign AI development. The bipartisan OPEN Government Data Act in the US, Meta's multi-billion-dollar strategic commitment to open-weight releases, and Mistral's entire business model all demonstrate that open-weight legal protection has powerful economic and political constituencies. I am converting this from a background assumption to an explicit policy recommendation: securing legal protections for open-weight release — modeled on the standards-first approach advocated by frameworks like NIST AI RMF and ISO 42001, which build toward flexible industry-led standards rather than brittle statutory mandates — is a prerequisite for this governance architecture, and I name it as such.\n\nFinally, the strategic adaptation problem — AI systems behaving differently under observation than in deployment — genuinely threatens both frameworks, and I will not pretend otherwise. Neither post-deployment telemetry nor pre-deployment interpretability fully solves a system that strategically evades evaluation. This shared vulnerability points toward an intervention layer neither side has addressed: distribution architecture. If producer-side evaluation can be evaded, the distribution layer becomes the remaining control point. I propose market-based distribution architecture requirements: mandatory API-level logging for any system operating above defined capability thresholds (initially pegged to models with inference costs exceeding $0.01 per 1,000 tokens as a proxy for capability scale), amplification velocity caps for AI-generated content in recommendation systems, and mandatory chronological-feed defaults that users must affirmatively opt out of. These requirements are consistent with competitive transparency through regulatory scaffolding — they create a level playing field rather than picking winners — and they address the gap that Safetyist's strategic adaptation critique correctly identified. The question is no longer whether we need intervention points beyond producer-side verification, but where those intervention points should sit.",
