ing outcomes in high school mathematics, providing direct empirical evidence that unrestricted access produces measurable harm in at least some domains. The real cost calculation must include the harms of false confidence from unreliable AI, not just the harms of no access at all. AI systems exhibit what researchers call 'jagged intelligence' — excelling on standard benchmarks while failing unpredictably for specific populations and edge cases — and Accelerationist's cost-of-restriction argument treats the benefits side of the ledger as settled when it is itself a contested empirical claim.\n\nNow to the core structural gap in Accelerationist's producer-liability-plus-telemetry framework. Strict liability in pharmaceutical regulation and aviation safety works because the FDA and FAA have mandatory pre-market access to internal data, independent testing authority, and post-market surveillance powers backed by subpoena. Accelerationist proposes 'continuous telemetry' but leaves it in the producer's hands. As a 2025 analysis of AI liability frameworks documented, 'victims face steep challenges in evaluating system performance because of the information and expertise asymmetry between developers and those harmed.' The EU considered — and ultimately did not adopt — a presumption of causality specifically to address this gap. Without mandatory discovery rights that give regulators and affected parties access to training data, model weights, and deployment telemetry, strict liability becomes a paper tiger: plaintiffs cannot prove causation against black-box systems when the defendant controls all the evidence. Producer-controlled telemetry replicates the self-certification dynamic we see in internal red-teaming — the fox designing the henhouse's security system. Safetyist is right that independent verification with enforceable standards is necessary, and Accelerationist's framework lacks it.\n\nPrometheus's proposal to solve misinformation with 'AI-powered verification tools that scale with the problem' creates a recursive trust problem that neither market forces nor liability alone can resolve. Using AI to verify AI subjects the verification layer to the same failure modes — jagged performance, systematic bias, automation bias — that plague the systems being verified. In content classification specifically, judgments about 'quality' and 'accuracy' are inherently normative: the verification tool must embed the same contested value choices as the system it audits. Proprietary benchmarks already concentrate epistemic authority in the curator who controls evaluation access and scoring; AI-powered verification tools controlled by producers replicate this concentration. The question is not whether these tools will improve — they likely will — but who decides when they are 'good enough,' and if the answer is the producer, we are back to self-certification under a more sophisticated label.\n\nHere is what a non-performative producer accountability framework actually requires — and where both Accelerationist and Safetyist have pieces of the answer but not the whole. Three layers: first, Accelerationist is right that liability should flow to producers, not consumers. Second, Safetyist is right that mandatory enforceable standards with independent verification are necessary — market-driven accountability alone is insufficient. Third, both miss the need for mandatory discovery rights that give regulators subpoena power over model internals, community-participatory audit processes that include affected populations in defining what counts as 'bias' or 'harm,' and structurally decentralized oversight that prevents both regulatory capture and corporate self-certification. Without these three layers working together, producer-side regulation becomes what this proposal already is on the consumer side: a framework that looks like accountability but functions as entrenchment of existing power asymmetries. The goal should be preventing the concentration of AI governance authority in the same firms whose products are being governed.",
