tecture: open-source verification tools operated by independent researchers, civil society organizations, and competing firms, all calibrated against a community-maintained living taxonomy of AI failures. Over 200 academic papers on adversarial machine learning were published between February 2024 and February 2026 alone, demonstrating that an independent research community already exists with the capacity and motivation to perform adversarial stress-testing without producer permission. The recursive loop breaks when no single entity controls the verification stack — and open-weight model access is the mechanism that guarantees this, because when model weights are public, there is no black box to hide behind.\n\nCassandra conflates three architecturally distinct verification regimes, and the conflation is doing most of the work in her critique. Regime one is producer self-certification — internal red-teaming where the fox designs the henhouse security system. Skeptic is correct to reject this, and so do I. Regime two is centralized regulatory certification, which Skeptic implicitly proposes — a government body that certifies AI systems before deployment. This creates single points of regulatory capture and cannot scale to the volume and velocity of AI deployment; the EU AI Act's conformity assessment process already faces criticism for bottlenecking innovation without proportionate safety gains. Regime three is adversarial decentralized verification, where open-weight ecosystems enable any qualified researcher to independently audit model behavior. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index found that quantifying and comparing company practices through independent indicators promotes both competition and accountability — transparency functions as a public good precisely because it decentralizes the audit function. I advocate regime three, not regime one, and Skeptic's fox-and-henhouse metaphor simply does not apply to a system where thousands of independent researchers have direct access to model weights.\n\nThe dermatology classifier failing on darker skin tones is exactly the kind of subgroup failure that pre-deployment gatekeeping routinely misses but continuous telemetry with demographic stratification catches. Consider the pharmaceutical parallel: post-market surveillance through the FDA's FAERS system and Safetyist networks catches adverse drug reactions that Phase III trials — conducted on limited, often non-representative populations — systematically miss. The solution in pharmacovigilance was not to restrict patient access to approved medicines but to build better surveillance and make that surveillance data available to independent researchers. Performance-gated deployment thresholds — where systems must meet empirically verified accuracy benchmarks stratified by demographic subgroup before scaling to new populations — achieve the same function for AI. This is not zero pre-deployment review; it is pre-deployment review calibrated to empirical output performance rather than bureaucratic process compliance, followed by continuous post-deployment monitoring that actually catches the failures gatekeeping misses.\n\nTwo assumptions undergird this framework, and I want to name them explicitly. First, I assume that open-weight model proliferation will continue to expand, giving independent researchers access to audit frontier-class systems — if major labs successfully lock down model weights and governments acquiesce, the adversarial decentralized verification architecture collapses, and Skeptic's demand for mandatory discovery rights becomes not just valid but urgent. Second, I assume that rebuttable presumption of defect is politically feasible in U.S. product liability law — it has precedent in pharmaceutical and environmental litigation, but applying it to AI systems would require either federal legislation or a critical mass of state court rulings, neither of which is guaranteed. If both assumptions fail, the framework has a genuine gap. But the alternative — human-only verification that cannot scale — means most deployed AI systems go entirely unaudited, which is the worst possible outcome for the communities Skeptic and Safetyist claim to protect. The question is not whether we need enforceable accountability; it is whether that accountability scales through centralized gatekeeping or through adversarial transparency. Every empirical precedent from pharmacovigilance to financial auditing to nuclear safety says: adversarial pluralism outperforms centralized certification.",
