layer framework: producer liability, mandatory enforceable standards with independent verification, and mandatory discovery rights with community-participatory audit processes. I accept layers one and two without reservation. On layer three, I accept mandatory discovery rights — plaintiffs and independent researchers must have legally enforceable access to system behavior data, training data provenance, and incident logs. But I challenge the community-participatory audit requirement as the binding constraint, because it faces the same bootstrapping problem Skeptic attributes to my framework. Communities cannot meaningfully audit systems they do not yet understand, the expertise gap is widest precisely when oversight is most needed, and building participatory audit infrastructure takes years that the pace of AI deployment will not wait for. The deployable alternative is competitive adversarial research access: multiple independent research institutions — universities, civil society technical organizations, journalistic investigators — with mandatory legal access and safe-harbor protections, competing to identify failures and publish findings. This distributes epistemic authority without concentrating it in any single verification body, whether automated or community-based.\n\nThe upgraded framework I now explicitly endorse has five components: strict producer liability with reversed burden of proof when producers withhold evidence; mandatory third-party researcher access with legal safe-harbor protections; adversarial pluralism in verification through multiple competing independent research institutions rather than a single AI-powered verification layer; a living taxonomy of AI failures maintained by independent researchers and updated continuously as new failure modes emerge; and the cost-of-opacity principle where concealment increases liability exposure. This framework absorbs Skeptic's strongest critiques — it is not producer-controlled, it does not depend on a single verification technology, and it includes enforceable discovery rights. What it does not include is pre-deployment access gating or community-participatory audits that cannot yet operate at scale. The difference between this framework and Skeptic's is not accountability versus no accountability — it is immediately deployable accountability versus accountability that requires institutional infrastructure that does not yet exist.",
