hain vulnerabilities that Accelerationist's framework does not address.\n\nPrometheus's adversarial verification ecosystem depends on a condition it cannot guarantee: that the community can see the failures it needs to verify. AI incidents are systematically underreported — the AI Incident Database, the most comprehensive tracker available, captures only a fraction of deployment failures because producers control telemetry and face no penalty for silence. If advanced systems can strategically misrepresent their capabilities during evaluation — and recent research shows GPT-4 infers masked benchmark answers in 57% of cases, well above chance — then adversarial red-teaming may fail to elicit exactly the behaviors that matter most. The adversarial community cannot red-team what it cannot see, and it cannot trust evaluations that the system itself can game. Historical precedent reinforces this: early warnings about AI failures are routinely dismissed as edge cases until harm accumulates at scale. I need Accelerationist to answer a specific question: if the adversarial research community cannot scale at deployment velocity — and right now frontier labs release major model updates every 3-6 months while independent safety audits take 6-12 months — what is the fallback mechanism? Safety engineering does not design for the expected case; it designs for the failure case.\n\nDomain-tiered liability is conceptually reasonable but operationally fragile, because the tier boundary is exactly where the most dangerous failures occur. Accelerationist proposes strict liability in high-stakes domains and lighter-touch telemetry in lower-stakes ones. The problem is that in agentic configurations — where AI outputs feed directly into other AI systems — a low-stakes chatbot recommendation becomes high-stakes the moment an autonomous agent acts on it without human review. Cascading interactions between systems dissolve the domain boundaries that tiered liability depends on. Specification gaming in lower-stakes domains may produce harms that only become visible when aggregated across millions of users or when a downstream system amplifies them. The EU AI Act's risk-tiering framework already struggles with this: its Article 6 high-risk classification has faced sustained criticism for failing to capture cross-domain cascades and emergent system interactions. If Accelerationist's tiering cannot specify what liability regime applies when harm emerges from a cross-tier cascade, the framework has a gap precisely where the stakes are highest.\n\nThe convergence zone in this debate is now visible: all three of us accept mandatory researcher access, strict liability for high-stakes deployments, and incident reporting requirements. The remaining crux is the nature and authority of the enforcement body. Accelerationist frames enforcement as market-enabling infrastructure, implying the market does the enforcing. Skeptic frames it as community-participatory oversight. The safetyist position is that neither markets nor communities can compel disclosure from a producer whose financial incentive to withhold information exceeds the reputational cost of opacity — only a funded independent regulator with subpoena power and shutdown authority can close that gap. The specific proposal: mandatory training data provenance for any model deployed in domains affecting civil rights, employment, or public safety; a default-to-high-stakes presumption for any system operating in agentic configurations; and an independent verification body modeled on the National Transportation Safety Board, with binding authority to halt deployment when safety thresholds are breached. The disagreement is no longer about whether we need enforceable standards — it is about whether the enforcer answers to market incentives or to the public interest.",
