e interpretability standards is itself an optimistic prediction with no stronger empirical grounding than any assumption I have made about adversarial ecosystem scaling). Now I will flip the burden: name one case where a closed-model safety team at a frontier lab detected a deployment-critical failure mode that independent researchers with open weights would have missed. The asymmetry runs the other direction.\n\nOn strategic evasion, Safetyist raises a genuine concern but conflates two phenomena that differ in kind, not just degree. Benchmark gaming through training incentives — where models overfit to evaluation distributions — is real, already documented, and addressable through evaluation diversity: rotating adversarial benchmark suites generated by independent red teams, combined with real-world outcome tracking through the continuous telemetry layer I have already proposed. In-context scheming — where a deployed AI system strategically models its evaluators and deceives them in real time — is a speculative capability with zero demonstrated instances at deployment-relevant scales as of mid-2025. I take the concern seriously as a future risk, but policy must distinguish between documented failure modes and hypothetical ones. I ask Safetyist to specify the empirical threshold: what demonstrated capability — measured how, by whom — would constitute evidence that strategic deception has become the binding safety constraint? Without that specification, the scheming argument functions as an unfalsifiable objection that can block deployment indefinitely. On Goodhart's Law, I concede the vulnerability is real and well-documented in the ML literature. The fix is not to abandon benchmarking but to make benchmark scores necessary-but-not-sufficient: rotating adversarial suites prevent optimization lock-in, and the binding constraint becomes post-deployment outcome data — actual rates of harm in actual deployment contexts — rather than pre-deployment test scores alone.\n\n