stematically favor novel, emotionally arousing, and polarizing content — AI-generated or not. Regulating distribution topology is a market-compatible intervention that targets the harm mechanism without gatekeeping creation tools: mandatory provenance labeling for AI-generated content, algorithmic amplification caps, and reshare friction requirements. This matters because 'misinformation' is an inherently unstable regulatory category vulnerable to political capture. Regulatory triggers should be defined as verifiable procedural failures — fabricated citations, synthetic media without provenance metadata, undisclosed AI generation in contexts where disclosure is material — rather than substantive truth judgments that require a government body to adjudicate what is true. Procedural violations are objectively verifiable, enforceable by existing consumer protection agencies like the FTC, and resistant to the epistemic authority problem that makes content-based regulation a tool of incumbents. As the 2025 sovereign AI survey found, 93% of respondents agree that open collaboration is essential for building secure and culturally aligned AI systems; restricting open-weight release does not make verification more robust — it makes verification dependent on the very entities whose incentives least align with transparency.",
