aults, API-level logging above capability thresholds — are structurally identical to binding regulatory mandates on platform design. Calling them \"market-enabling infrastructure\" does not change what they are. The real remaining disagreement is not whether binding standards are needed — all three of us now accept that — but who holds the authority to set, enforce, and update them, and whether affected communities have standing in that process.\n\nThe Zou et al. case — correctly attributed to Google DeepMind researchers working on Vicuna and LLaMA 1, not Llama 2 — demonstrates something real but narrower than Accelerationist claims. Open-weight access enabled discovery of adversarial suffix attacks, which is valuable red-teaming. But vulnerability discovery is not comprehensive safety verification. AI systems exhibit what researchers call jagged intelligence — high performance in tested domains coexisting with catastrophic failure in adjacent untested ones — and finding one class of adversarial exploit tells us nothing about failure modes in dimensions nobody thought to test. Training data composition, deployment context effects, and population-level impact patterns remain invisible even with full weight access. Open weights enable attack discovery; they do not enable the systematic safety understanding that governance requires. I partially credit Accelerationist's evidence here while noting it covers one dimension of the transparency problem my framework addresses across four.\n\nFinally, all three frameworks share a vulnerability none of us has squarely addressed: \"misinformation\" is not a stable regulatory category, and any governance architecture that requires someone to determine what counts as misinformation will either collapse into censorship or paralysis. Over 1,500 AI-related bills were introduced in state legislatures in the past year, and many of them founder on exactly this definitional problem. The way forward is to define regulatory triggers as procedural failures rather than substantive truth judgments: fabricated citations, undisclosed AI generation, synthetic media distributed without provenance markers. These are verifiable, enforceable, and sidestep First Amendment challenges because they regulate process transparency, not content. My framework can absorb distribution architecture as a fourth layer — governing how AI-generated content moves through information ecosystems — if we anchor enforcement in procedural obligations rather than truth adjudication. This is the frontier where the three of us should be competing, not relitigating whether binding standards are needed.",
