rds-setting. This is not a thought experiment; it is a design pattern with forty years of operational history across environmental, financial, and urban planning regulation.\n\nNow let me score Accelerationist's updated framework against my three layers. Layer 1, producer liability with mandatory discovery: Accelerationist now accepts pre-market data submission and subpoena power — this substantially satisfies Layer 1. Layer 2, enforceable standards with independent verification: Accelerationist accepts binding halt authority and continuous monitoring, which partially satisfies Layer 2, but the bidirectional liability mechanism with a 30-day justification window and 1.5x damages multiplier for \"politically motivated\" halts structurally undermines it. The FAA issues airworthiness directives grounding entire aircraft fleets without owing Boeing or Airbus compensation if the directive proves overcautious — as happened with the 737 MAX grounding in March 2019, which lasted 20 months and cost Boeing an estimated $20 billion, none of it recoverable from the FAA. The FDA recalled Vioxx without compensating Merck for $2.5 billion in lost annual revenue. Accelerationist's framework inverts this established principle by making the regulator the defendant. The International AI Safety Report (2025) documents that various entities have adopted frontier AI safety frameworks but \"there is no unified approach at this time\" — adding litigation risk to under-resourced regulators who attempt to enforce halt authority will not unify that approach; it will paralyze it. Layer 3, community-participatory audits: Accelerationist has not addressed this layer at all. Net assessment: Accelerationist moved from roughly 30% to 55% of adequate — the bidirectional liability mechanism is not a detail to negotiate later; it is the load-bearing joint that determines whether halt authority is a safety tool or a decorative one.\n\nOn the Zou et al. case: the factual corrections matter beyond academic accuracy. The research involved Google DeepMind-affiliated scientists at elite institutions with substantial computational resources — not the affected communities or independent watchdogs that open-weight advocates invoke when arguing for democratized verification. Finding one vulnerability class, adversarial suffixes, is genuine and valuable adversarial research, but it does not constitute the systematic safety verification that deployment in healthcare, criminal justice, or financial services requires. The case demonstrates that open weights enable a specific kind of security research by a specific kind of actor. That is worth preserving. It is not evidence that open weights deliver the broad-based, community-accessible transparency my framework requires — a distinction the debate on open weights versus genuine transparency must maintain.\n\nFinally, all three of our frameworks share an unaddressed structural problem: \"misinformation\" is an unstable regulatory category. Strict liability for AI-generated misinformation, safety standards for misinformation classification, and community audits of misinformation harms all require someone to adjudicate what counts as misinformation — a definitional challenge that has defeated every prior regulatory attempt, from the Fairness Doctrine to Section 230 reform. The workable regulatory trigger is verifiable procedural failures rather than substantive truth judgments: fabricated citations that can be checked against source databases, synthetic media distributed without provenance markers detectable through C2PA-standard metadata, and undisclosed AI generation where disclosure is legally required. Detection at scale is feasible for these categories because they involve verifiable artifacts — a citation either exists or it doesn't, a provenance marker is either present or it isn't — unlike content-level truth judgments that require contextual interpretation. False-positive costs are bounded because the triggers are binary