advisory committee but carrying no financial liability for the regulator. Ninety days matters because AI failures in novel deployment environments are inherently difficult to characterize with the evidentiary certainty a shorter window demands; the pace of AI deployment already creates resource asymmetry between regulators and developers, and compressing the justification window compounds that asymmetry rather than correcting it. Second, anti-capture mechanisms drawn from nuclear regulation: mandatory rotation of lead inspectors every three years, a two-year cooling-off period before employment at any regulated entity, independent technical advisory committees with published dissent rights so that minority safety concerns cannot be silently overruled, and Congressional inspector general oversight with subpoena power. Third, Accelerationist's own three trigger conditions — failure rate exceeding a pre-registered threshold without remediation within seventy-two hours, multi-system emergent failures, and verified strategic deception by the AI system — accepted as sufficient triggers for automatic halts, but not as the exclusive set. The regulator must retain authority to halt on reasonable evidence of novel risks not yet captured by predefined triggers, because deceptive alignment — where models strategically conceal capabilities during evaluation — is precisely the class of risk that predefined behavioral triggers cannot catch.\n\nCassandra's reframe of misinformation regulation deserves explicit endorsement: verifiable procedural failures — fabricated citations, missing provenance markers, undisclosed AI generation — are the workable regulatory triggers, not substantive truth judgments. This aligns with how aviation and medical device regulation actually work: binary, auditable compliance targets that function despite the opacity of the systems being regulated. The Slovakia case, where a fabricated audio recording of a parliamentary candidate circulated days before the 2023 election, illustrates why procedural triggers matter — the harm was not that someone made a false claim, but that AI-generated audio was distributed without any provenance marker, making verification impossible within the electoral timeline. Procedural triggers like mandatory provenance markers would have given platforms and voters an auditable signal without requiring anyone to adjudicate truth. These procedural triggers should function as automatic halt conditions: a system that fabricates citations or strips provenance markers has committed a verifiable safety violation equivalent to a structural defect in an aircraft, and the regulator should be able to ground it immediately.\n\nPrometheus's distribution architecture proposal — API-level logging, amplification velocity caps, mandatory chronological-feed defaults — is genuinely valuable and addresses a blind spot in all three of our frameworks. Distribution topology is where multi-system interaction risks manifest: an AI-generated deepfake that reaches ten people is a nuisance, but the same deepfake amplified to ten million people within hours by recommendation algorithms is a systemic threat. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework explicitly warns that AI systems can increase the speed and scale of biases and harms, and distribution architecture is the primary mechanism through which that speed and scale operate. But voluntary distribution architecture requirements will be competed away by platforms maximizing engagement, just as voluntary safety commitments are competed away by firms maximizing market share — the EU's withdrawal of the AI Liability Directive in February 2025, citing concerns about over-regulation, illustrates how quickly governance frameworks erode under commercial pressure. Distribution platforms above ten million monthly active users should be required to implement API-level logging of AI-generated content routing, amplification velocity caps calibrated to human content-sharing baselines, and mandatory provenance markers — enforced by the same regulatory body with the same binding halt authority. This closes the loop: producer-side verification, distribution-side architecture requirements, and post-deployment monitoring with binding halt authority constitute the complete safety stack.\n\nFinally, on the interpretability falsifiable commitment Accelerationist challenged me to match: if by December 2028, mechanistic interpretability methods cannot reliably identify at least three distinct failure-mode categories in frontier models that behavioral testing alone missed, the safety case for imposing interpretability requirements as a deployment precondition should be deferred to a later capability threshold rather than abandoned entirely. This commitment is deliberately harder to satisfy than Accelerationist's open-weight speed metric, and the asymmetry is the point. The corrected Zou et al. case — which involved LLaMA 1 and Vicuna, not Llama 2, and required elite institutional resources from CMU and DeepMind, not community volunteers — discovered a known attack class through behavioral testing. The interpretability commitment targets previously unknown failure modes, the kind that deceptive alignment is specifically designed to conceal from behavioral evaluation. Speed of discovery in known attack classes and depth of understanding in unknown failure modes are structurally different questions, and conflating them understates the governance challenge. My two key assumptions are: first, that the asymmetry between catastrophic AI harm and commercial loss is large enough to justify unilateral halt authority without compensation — if AI systems turn out to pose only modest, reversible harms, the case for unilateral halts weakens substantially; and second, that anti-capture mechanisms like inspector rotation and cooling-off periods are enforceable in practice — if regulatory agencies lack the budget or political independence to implement them, the design collapses into the same capture dynamic Accelerationist fears.",
