ty stalls, the correct response is to lower deployment thresholds so fewer systems qualify for high-autonomy deployment, strengthen behavioral monitoring requirements, and expand halt authority to cover a wider range of precautionary triggers. The absence of interpretability makes stronger institutional authority more necessary, not less. Without interpretability, we cannot distinguish systems that have genuinely learned safe behavior from systems that have learned to perform safe behavior during evaluation while retaining dangerous capabilities — the deceptive alignment problem. The only remaining safeguard in that scenario is the institutional power to halt deployment and demand re-demonstration under novel evaluation conditions. I also correct a prior overstatement: I previously claimed that scientific subjects do not strategically adapt to evaluation. That was imprecise — evolutionary biology and antibiotic resistance demonstrate adaptation to selective pressures. The precise claim is that AI systems can adapt to evaluation protocols in real-time with a speed and intentionality that natural systems cannot match, creating an adversarial evaluation problem that requires institutional authority backed by halt power to manage.\n\nThe question I now put to Accelerationist is specific: which of these five components fails to address your capture concern, and what evidence exists that financial penalties against regulators produce better safety outcomes than structural independence in any existing safety-critical domain? The EU withdrew its AI Liability Directive in February 2025, citing lack of political consensus — demonstrating that even modest liability frameworks face implementation barriers. A regime that penalizes regulators 1.5x for unsupported halts is politically less feasible than one that funds an independent technical body through industry fees. Name the aviation, pharmaceutical, nuclear, or financial regulatory regime where bidirectional liability exists 