al review. Bad-faith halts trigger personnel consequences and public reporting, not damages paid to the regulated entity. This design handles AI's unique velocity problem — tier one automatic triggers operate faster than any human decision process — while preserving the asymmetric authority that every successful safety regime requires.\n\nTwo extensions build coalition here. I endorse Skeptic's reframe of misinformation regulation around procedural triggers rather than content-level truth judgments. Fabricated citations, missing provenance markers, and undisclosed AI generation are verifiable, auditable, binary checks — they avoid the mathematical impossibility of content-level truth adjudication while remaining enforceable. The Slovakia 2023 case, where a fabricated audio recording of a candidate circulated days before parliamentary elections, illustrates exactly the kind of harm that provenance-marking requirements would address without requiring regulators to become truth arbiters. These procedural triggers belong in tier one of the halt architecture as automatic circuit-breaker conditions. I also reframe Accelerationist's distribution architecture proposals — API logging, amplification velocity caps, chronological-feed defaults — as safety-critical infrastructure requirements that need binding regulatory authority rather than voluntary market adoption. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework already identifies speed and scale of AI-amplified harms as a governance concern; recommendation algorithms are themselves multi-agent systems creating emergent amplification effects that no single producer controls, and the velocity of AI-generated content through distribution systems is precisely the mechanism that collapses governance visibility. Voluntary adoption of distribution controls will fail for the same reason voluntary adoption of emissions controls failed: competitive pressure punishes the first mover.\n\nMy argument depends on two key assumptions. First, that st